\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Conjecture}
\title{A discrete induced arithmetic theory}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{The discrete induced theory is an arithmetic scheme equipped with a
Clifford sheaf and a finite group action; the continuum limit and the
background dependence are controlled by a map from a moduli stack;
the numerical parameters of the resulting QFT are special values of
L-functions attached to the arithmetic data.}

\p{This is a schematic research conjecture. An
[arithmetic family of forms](fdip-000U), its
[Clifford algebra sheaf](fdip-000V) and a specified
[finite-group action](fdip-000J) are available finite constructions.
The moduli-stack clause is an informal target for organizing families,
isomorphisms and descent; no site, stack or continuum comparison map
has been specified here. Nor has an arithmetic L-function or its
special value been identified with a parameter of a quantum field
theory (QFT). These terms describe the conjectured interfaces, not
additional structures carried by every finite Gaussian model.}

\p{The first background-selection question is algebraic. Schematically,
let #{B_{\mathrm{alg}}} denote a chosen set of algebraic background
data and #{\mathcal S} a chosen set of admissible structures; a
selector would be a map
##{\sigma:B_{\mathrm{alg}}\longrightarrow\mathcal S.}
A physical interpretation would additionally require a set
#{\operatorname{Env}_{\mathrm{phys}}} of physical environments and a map
##{E:\operatorname{Env}_{\mathrm{phys}}\longrightarrow B_{\mathrm{alg}}.}
Neither map is asserted to have been constructed by the
[enriched-background example](fdip-000X). The
[finite interfaces and their limits](fdip-003Y) specify what the
present calculations do establish.}
